1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:06,800
Koffi’s butcher shop: food safety rules in the WAEMU area.

2
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:10,800
This is Koffi, a butcher at the city market.

3
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:17,800
His meat comes from herders, passes through the livestock market and the abattoir, then reaches his customers.

4
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:28,600
In WAEMU, Regulation No. 007/2007/CM/UEMOA sets common rules for the safety of plants, animals and food.

5
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:33,400
Every food business in the chain answers for the safety of what it sells.

6
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:41,000
The regulation rests on common principles: measures follow international standards.

7
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:47,400
Risks are assessed on scientific data, and measures stay in proportion to them.

8
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:54,000
Faced with a risk of serious harm, a lack of full scientific certainty is no excuse to wait.

9
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,200
And risks are explained to consumers and professionals.

10
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:07,000
Who does what? Under the WAEMU Commission, a regional committee advises, backed by the Veterinary Committee.

11
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:13,400
Networks of experts, laboratories and alerts link the member states.

12
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:19,800
Each state has a veterinary administration and a national food safety body,

13
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:25,200
and an advisory council where consumers also have a seat.

14
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:31,800
A trader offers Koffi cheaper meat with no inspection stamp. What does Koffi do?

15
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:38,600
Answer 2: only buy inspected meat, with the abattoir stamp.

16
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:43,400
Koffi buys inspected meat at the abattoir, with its stamp.

17
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:49,200
In his notebook he records every purchase: the date, the supplier, where the animals came from.

18
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:54,000
That is traceability: knowing where a product comes from and where it goes.

19
00:01:54,600 --> 00:02:01,900
The rule is clear: food businesses answer for what they sell, and sell only safe products.

20
00:02:01,900 --> 00:02:09,900
Each one checks its own operations at every stage: production, processing, storage and distribution.

21
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:17,900
And each must be able to show inspectors its own checks and the trade records for its food.

22
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:23,900
One day, the veterinary services issue an alert about a batch of meat.

23
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:30,300
Thanks to the notebook, the batch is found and withdrawn. The rest of the market carries on.

24
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:36,700
In WAEMU, member states cooperate and share alerts to protect consumers.

25
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:46,400
If a business thinks a food it has sold may harm health, it must inform the authorities and act to protect consumers.

26
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:53,400
In serious danger, the national food safety body can suspend sales, withdraw the food or have it destroyed.

27
00:02:53,400 --> 00:03:00,600
In an emergency, local authorities can act for up to a month, informing that body within 24 hours.

28
00:03:01,300 --> 00:03:08,600
In 2009, WAEMU listed the animal diseases that must be notified: more than one hundred and twenty.

29
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:16,600
Among them: foot-and-mouth disease, contagious bovine pleuropneumonia, peste des petits ruminants and anthrax.

30
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:24,600
One morning, a herder offers Koffi cattle that are drooling, with sores in their mouths.

31
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:29,600
What does Koffi do?

32
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,800
Answer 2: he refuses and alerts the veterinary services.

33
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:45,600
Any suspicion must be brought to the veterinary authority at once.

34
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:53,600
The state notifies the WAEMU Commission immediately, in writing, on WOAH’s form.

35
00:03:53,600 --> 00:04:01,600
It declares the infection and applies measures within a defined area.

36
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:09,600
In a health crisis, states warn the Commission and each other at once through the alert network.

37
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:15,600
They take safeguard measures, and inform WOAH when animals are affected.

38
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:19,600
The measures end once the crisis is eradicated.

39
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:27,600
Animals sent to another member state travel with a veterinary certificate from an official veterinarian.

40
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:35,400
Meat and animal products entering the Union carry a health certificate: healthy animals, hygienic handling.

41
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:43,600
In an epizootic, a state can restrict animals from just the affected parts of another state.

42
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:51,600
Food and animal products that meet the rules move freely across the Union.

43
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:59,000
Any business can sell in another member state the products it sells lawfully in its own.

44
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:07,600
And rules must stay coherent, simple and transparent, with no needless barriers to trade.

45
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:15,000
And when Koffi’s shop is inspected, he is entitled to transparency, impartiality and proportionality.

46
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:21,600
States give the people concerned a say and tell them about risks and the measures taken.

47
00:05:21,600 --> 00:05:27,600
Awareness campaigns, like this one, help everyone detect and prevent health risks.

48
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:49,600
Four rules: safe products, good records, informing the authorities and reporting disease.

49
00:06:50,300 --> 00:06:59,000
A safe chain, from herd to plate.
